{"author":"rebar review bot","author_email":"rebar-review-bot@rebar.solutions.navateam.com","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\nT1 (prior art) is the only plan-review criterion that searches the web. When that search\nreturns nothing, the underlying `ddgs` library RAISES instead of returning an empty list.\nrebar has no handler, so the exception escapes the tool, is classified as an LLM provider\noutage, and degrades the ENTIRE plan review to an unsigned `INDETERMINATE` verdict. A\nreview costing roughly 8 minutes of LLM time is discarded because a search came back\nempty.\n\n## Root cause (confirmed by reproduction)\n\nCall chain, at current tree lines:\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/runner.py:502` sets `web_requested`; `:522` calls `web_search_capabilities`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/capabilities.py:154-159` builds `WebSearch(native=..., local=duckduckgo_search_tool(...))`, passing pydantic-ai's tool straight through.\n- `pydantic_ai/common_tools/duckduckgo.py:60` awaits the client with NO exception handler.\n- `ddgs/ddgs.py:454` does `raise DDGSException(err or \"No results found.\")`.\n\n`grep -rn 'DDGSException|ddgs' src/rebar/` returns nothing: rebar has no handler anywhere.\n\nReproduced live, 2 failures in 3 attempts at `ddgs.py:454`. A backend probe showed `auto`\nand `bing` returning results while `duckduckgo`, `google`, `brave`, and `mojeek` raised;\na synthetic zero-result query made every backend raise 3/3.\n\nrebar's classifier returns `NEEDS_INVESTIGATION`, `retryable=False`, raising\n`LLMUnavailableError`. `gate_dispatch.py:204` catches it and calls\n`_degraded_plan_review_verdict` with `llm_ran=False`. There is no retry, no per-criterion\nfallback, and no no-web review path.\n\n## The trap this fix must avoid\n\nA controlled probe proved a genuinely empty result and a failed backend are\nINDISTINGUISHABLE in the common case: both raise `DDGSException(\"No results found.\")` with\nthe same type, same message, and no `__cause__` or `__context__`. Only a distinctive\nmessage, such as a timeout or backend-specific text, can be told apart.\n\nSo a handler that swallows the exception and returns `[]` is NOT a fix: it would make T1\nconclude \"no prior art exists\" when the search actually failed, trading a loud failure for\na quiet wrong answer.\n\n## Impact\n\nEight open bugs are instances of this defect, each with an identical session-log payload\nnaming `DDGSException`: `0b56-ad83`, `2b30-5d6f`, `2ced-55f3`, `602b-4a55`, `812d-7eba`,\n`8d53-4d0f`, `cb9c-61c8`, `ccd8-e843`.\n\n## Approach (decided)\n\nOwn the local web-search boundary inside rebar instead of passing pydantic-ai's raw tool\nthrough. In `src/rebar/llm/capabilities.py`, `web_search_capabilities()` builds a\nrebar-owned local search callable wrapping the client, catching\n`ddgs.exceptions.DDGSException` at that boundary only.\n\nBehaviour on catch, split by what is knowable:\n\n1. Message exactly `\"No results found.\"` - indistinguishable between true-empty and\n   backend-failed, so return an empty list. T1 reasons from \"no prior-art results were\n   returned\" and the review COMPLETES instead of being voided.\n2. Any other message, including timeouts and backend-specific errors - return a list\n   holding exactly one degraded MARKER record, described below.\n\nNeither branch re-raises, so no web-search outcome can void a whole plan review again.\n\n### The marker record, and where its interpretation protocol lives\n\npydantic-ai's tool declares its return type as `list[DuckDuckGoResult]`, a `TypedDict` of\nthree `str` fields - `title`, `href`, `body` - validated through\n`TypeAdapter(list[DuckDuckGoResult])`. The marker is not a new schema; it is one ordinary\nrecord of that shape, so nothing downstream learns a new type:\n\n- `title` = `WEB_SEARCH_UNAVAILABLE` - a reserved, greppable sentinel.\n- `href` = `\"\"` - empty, so it can never be cited as a prior-art source.\n- `body` = text carrying BOTH the failure and the decision rule the reader must apply,\n  e.g. `Web search failed: <backend message>. This is a tool-failure notice, not a search\n  result. Prior art could not be verified, so do not conclude that none exists.`\n\nThe blocking finding on the previous revision was that the marker's meaning depended on a\nreader-side instruction this change did not add. The protocol is therefore CO-LOCATED WITH\nTHE PAYLOAD rather than placed in T1's prompt: the record states in its own `body` what it\nis and how to treat it, so it is self-describing to any reader. That is what keeps T1's\nprompt legitimately out of scope.\n\n### Alternatives considered for branch 2\n\n- RETRY on a timeout before emitting the marker. Rejected here: the probe showed `auto`\n  and `bing` succeeding while four backends raised on the SAME query, so retry is really\n  backend fallback, carrying its own policy questions (budget, ordering, whether a\n  differing second answer is trustworthy) and out of scope below. The marker is strictly\n  better than today meanwhile and does not foreclose adding retry in front of it later.\n- Classify timeouts as branch 1 (empty list). Rejected: a timeout IS distinguishable from\n  `\"No results found.\"`, so collapsing it would recreate the quiet-wrong-answer trap.\n- RAISE a rebar-specific typed error instead. Rejected: any raise from the tool path\n  re-enters the same `LLMUnavailableError` classification at `gate_dispatch.py:204` and\n  voids the whole review, which is the defect itself.\n\nThis is an in-process change on the tool path with no dependency on any CI provider, so it\nbehaves identically under `make verify`, GitHub Actions, and any other or no CI provider.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/capabilities.py` - the wrapper and its use in `web_search_capabilities`.\n- `tests/unit/test_web_tool_gating.py` - the red/green tests.\n\nOut of scope: retry or fallback across backends, caching prior-art results, and\nre-triaging the eight related tickets (handled separately once this lands).\n\nT1's prompt and its `suppress_types` are out of scope deliberately: the marker carries its\nown interpretation rule in `body`, so this change is complete on its own and needs no\ncoordinated prompt edit to land with it.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 When the client raises `DDGSException(\"No results found.\")`, rebar's local web-search tool RETURNS an empty list rather than raising. Checked: a test raises exactly that from the client and asserts an empty list comes back and nothing propagates.\n- [ ] AC2 On a `DDGSException` with any other message the tool returns exactly one marker record, not raising and not returning an empty list: `title` is exactly `WEB_SEARCH_UNAVAILABLE`, `href` is the empty string, and `body` contains BOTH the backend message AND an explicit instruction not to conclude that no prior art exists. Checked: a test raises `DDGSException(\"backend exploded\")` from the client and asserts all four, including `backend exploded` in `body`.\n- [ ] AC3 The marker validates against pydantic-ai's own return schema, so no consumer sees an unexpected shape. Checked: a test passes the return value of both branches through `TypeAdapter(list[DuckDuckGoResult])` from `pydantic_ai.common_tools.duckduckgo` and asserts each validates.\n- [ ] AC4 `web_search_capabilities(web=True)` installs rebar's wrapper as the local tool, not pydantic-ai's raw `duckduckgo_search_tool`, so the handler cannot be bypassed. Checked: a test asserts the identity or provenance of the installed local tool.\n- [ ] AC5 The new tests FAIL before the change and PASS after it. Checked: capture the red output naming the propagated `DDGSException`, then the green output.\n- [ ] AC6 No test here performs a live network request. Checked: every test monkeypatches the client; the suite passes with networking unavailable.\n- [ ] AC7 `make verify` shows no failure beyond the 17 known macOS-arm64 pandoc `Bad CPU type` failures, which are environmental.\n- [ ] AC8 The change lands on `main` through Gerrit with `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`.\n\n## Testing\n\nTargeted `pytest tests/unit/test_web_tool_gating.py`, then `make verify` in the change's\nown worktree. Expect exactly 17 pre-existing pandoc failures on macOS arm64; an 18th is\nreal and blocks the push."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789954794259836001,"uuid":"edd2217d-7a3f-45cb-ac81-eccc42e4d9f3"}